title: Checkout architecture
description: How an order flows from the storefront to fulfilment.

customer: Customer [person]
stripe: Stripe [cloud, purple, detail: payments]
mail: E-mail provider [cloud]
note: Every write carries an\nidempotency key [sticky]

group edge: Edge [gray] {
  web: Storefront [rounded, detail: Next.js]
  gateway: API gateway [hexagon]
}
group backend: Backend [blue] {
  orders: Orders API [detail: Rust · Axum]
  payments: Payments [detail: Rust]
  queue: Job queue [pill]
  worker: Fulfilment worker
  db: Postgres [cylinder]
}

customer -> web: browses
web -> gateway: HTTPS
gateway -> orders: POST /orders
orders -> payments: charge
payments -> stripe: PaymentIntent
orders -> db
orders ..> queue: order.created
queue ..> worker
worker -> mail: receipt
worker -> db

comment on payments by deyan: Should payments own its own database?
reply c1 by ana: Not before launch; a separate schema is enough.
comment on queue->worker by ana: What happens when the worker crashes mid-order?
comment c3 at 40,-60 by deyan: Add the admin dashboard to this picture
